  <h1>Types of Defamation Claims</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by Aspen Lockwood (AL), Editor-in-Chief. Updated May 2026.</em></p>
  <h2>Libel vs. slander</h2><p>Libel covers written, printed, or recorded false statements. Slander covers spoken, transitory ones. Libel is typically easier to prove (the record exists) and courts historically treated it as more severe. In the digital age, social media posts, emails, and videos are libel &mdash; not slander.</p>
  <h2>Per se vs. per quod</h2><p>Per se defamation: the statement is defamatory on its face, without needing extrinsic evidence. Damages are presumed. Per quod: the statement appears innocent on its face but is defamatory in context (e.g., a photo captioned with false information). Per quod requires proof of special (economic) damages.</p>
  <h2>Trade libel</h2><p>False statements about a business&rsquo;s products or services that harm economic interests. Distinct from personal defamation &mdash; the false statement must concern the goods/services, not the owner. Requires proof of actual economic loss and is harder to prove than personal defamation.</p>
  <h2>Defenses</h2><p>Truth is an absolute defense &mdash; true statements cannot be defamatory. Opinion is protected if it does not imply false facts. Privilege (absolute for legislative/judicial proceedings; qualified for employer references or statements in the public interest) may protect the defendant even if the statement is false.</p>
